---
<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  h1={h1}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  related={related}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Home", url: "/" },
    { name: "Disclosure laws", url: "/laws/" },
    { name: "California rentals", url: path },
  ]}
  schema={schema}
>
  <p>
    From January 1, 2027, anyone advertising a California rental with a digitally altered
    or AI-generated photo must put a disclosure on or next to the image saying it has been
    altered, and must include the original photo in the ad or link to it. That's AB 2025,
    signed September 27, 2026, which adds Civil Code § 1940.11. It extends AB 723, which
    only covered sales, and it applies to landlords and property managers, not just
    real estate agents.
  </p>

  <h2>What § 1940.11 requires</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>A disclosure that the image has been altered</strong>, "reasonably
      conspicuous and located on or adjacent to the image."
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The original.</strong> The ad must include the unaltered version of the
      images in the posting, or a link, URL or QR code to a publicly accessible website
      that includes and clearly identifies the original. If you use a link, the disclosure
      has to say the unaltered images can be accessed through it.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What counts as altered</h2>
  <p>Two kinds of image are covered:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Edited photos</strong>: an existing image altered with editing software or
      AI to add, remove or change elements. The list is the same as the sales law's:
      fixtures, furniture, appliances, flooring, walls, paint color, hardscape, landscape,
      façade, floor plans, and things outside or visible from the property such as
      streetlights, utility poles, views through windows and neighboring properties.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fully AI-generated images</strong>, made by or at the direction of a person.
      This is new; the sales law has no equivalent. These still need the disclosure, but the
      original-image requirement doesn't apply to an image, architectural rendering or
      artistic rendering that wasn't originally created with a capture device.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Excluded: images where only lighting, sharpening, white balance, color correction,
    angle, straightening, cropping, exposure or other common adjustments were made that
    don't change the representation of the property.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it applies to</h2>
  <p>
    The sales law, § 10140.8, binds real estate brokers and salespersons and people acting
    for them. The rental law starts with "a person who includes a digitally altered image"
    in a rental ad. On its face that covers owners advertising their own units, property
    managers, and leasing staff. For licensees, a violation is also a violation of their
    licensing law.
  </p>

  <h2>What AB 2025 changes in the sales law</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Long leases move.</strong> New § 10140.8(c): the sales law "does not apply to
      an advertisement or other promotional material for a leasehold exceeding one year's
      duration." Those ads now follow § 1940.11.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wording of the link requirement.</strong> § 10140.8(a)(1) now reads "a link,
      URL, or QR code to a publicly accessible internet website" that includes the
      original, and the statement must say the originals "can be accessed via the link,
      URL, or QR code."
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <h3>Apartment listing with a virtually staged living room</h3>
  <p>
    Caption on the staged photo: <em>"Virtually staged — furniture added digitally. The
    unaltered photo is in this listing (photo 6)."</em> Then include the empty-room photo
    in the same listing. If the platform limits photos, link to a public page with the
    original instead and say so in the caption.
  </p>
  <h3>AI-generated "lifestyle" image of a unit that was never photographed that way</h3>
  <p>
    Label it as AI-generated or altered on or next to the image. No original is required,
    because it wasn't made with a camera. A rendering of a unit under construction falls
    in the same category.
  </p>
  <h3>Building's stock photo with a replaced window view: fails</h3>
  <p>
    Views through windows are on the list. Using an edited view without a disclosure and
    the original breaks the rule, even when the same image has run for years. It is also the
    pattern DC's bill sponsor singled out: stock photos of generic rooms that misrepresent
    window views and unit size.
  </p>

  <h2>Before January 1, 2027</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Audit active rental listings and evergreen building photos for edits beyond exposure and color.</li>
    <li>Keep the unedited originals somewhere public and stable, so a link still works months later.</li>
    <li>Add a disclosure line to every altered or AI-generated image, on or next to it, not only in the description.</li>
    <li>For listings over one year, follow the rental rule, not AB 723.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/tools/disclosure-generator/">disclosure generator</a> writes the
    statement for edited photos.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  {faq.map((f) => (
    <>
